1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,380
 Hey everyone, this is Ryan here and welcome back to our prosthodontic series.

2
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:12,060
 In this video, we're going to unpack the idea and the concepts behind Pontic and Connector Design.

3
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:16,960
 So we've already talked about the components of a bridge.

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:22,540
 Just to review the abutment is the tooth to which the bridge attaches.

5
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:29,380
 So these two abutment teeth have been properly prepped to receive the crowns of a bridge.

6
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:35,020
 The retainer refers to the crown that actually attaches to the abutment.

7
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:38,820
 So these two would be considered retainers.

8
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:48,940
 The Pontic is the fake tooth between abutments and the connector connects the retainer to the Pontic.

9
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:54,820
 So usually you'll have a Pontic or multiple Pontics between retainers.

10
00:00:54,820 --> 00:01:02,420
 But there are cases if you have what's called a cantilever bridge, you can have just one retainer

11
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:08,060
 and one or more Pontics attached to it.

12
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:13,240
 So you can have different combinations of these elements.

13
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:20,820
 So let's focus on the Pontic and the different designs that these fake teeth can have.

14
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:27,900
 So the first one is the hygienic Pontic, also sometimes called the sanitary Pontic.

15
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:33,220
 And this is a cross section or side view of this design.

16
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:39,020
 And notice the space between the Pontic and the ridge.

17
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:45,380
 So the hygienic Pontic is used primarily in the posterior mandible region.

18
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:51,020
 It offers great hygienic capability and hence the name.

19
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:55,540
 And the reason for that is because there's space between the Pontic and the ridge.

20
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:59,020
 So you can fit a round brush between this.

21
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:03,940
 You can fit super floss easily and you can keep food and other things from getting caught

22
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:06,460
 between the bridge and the gingiva.

23
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:09,940
 So that's the main benefit of this design.

24
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:15,060
 However, they're poor aesthetics because you have a pretty big gaping hole between the

25
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,180
 tooth and the gums.

26
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:23,940
 And you need to have enough video, which is a vertical dimension of occlusion or restorative

27
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:30,420
 space, meaning there has to be enough room to afford this extra two millimeters of space.

28
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:35,380
 Otherwise you're going to have to make this tooth, or the Pontic, very, very thin.

29
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:42,380
 And when you make something in dentistry too thin, it's prone to breaking fracture.

30
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:49,460
 So the second Pontic design is the saddle Pontic or the ridge lap Pontic.

31
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:52,780
 And this one you really should never use.

32
00:02:52,780 --> 00:03:00,180
 And it's because like a saddle on a horse, it firmly wraps around the ridge here.

33
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:06,820
 And so there's no way you could fit some kind of brush underneath that and keep it clean.

34
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:10,580
 And it's very bad for hygiene for that reason.

35
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:15,820
 So a lot of things can get caught under there and it's very hard to keep the area clean.

36
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:22,020
 It can lead to periodontal problems around the abutment teeth and eventual failure of

37
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:23,020
 the bridge.

38
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:28,860
 So this one is not often used and in fact you should never use it.

39
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:32,780
 The next design we have is the conical Pontic design.

40
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:35,940
 This one is reserved mostly for molars.

41
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:41,020
 And it's similar to the hygienic design but marginally better aesthetics.

42
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:48,020
 So the conical design you can see has a conical shape to it from the side view.

43
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:56,340
 And instead of having this space, it has just one little point of contact at the ridge.

44
00:03:56,340 --> 00:03:59,500
 So this enables you to slide a brush under here.

45
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:05,300
 There's some give of the soft tissue so you can put a brush or some super floss underneath

46
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:08,020
 the bridge and keep it clean.

47
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:09,620
 So this one's pretty good.

48
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:16,140
 It has some pros and maybe it's not as hygienic as the hygienic Pontic but it still offers

49
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:21,540
 ability for cleanseability.

50
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:27,100
 Alright so we talked about the ridge lap or saddle design, this is a modified form of

51
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:28,100
 it.

52
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,460
 So we call it the modified ridge lap Pontic.

53
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:38,220
 This is reserved mostly for anterior Pontics and it offers really good aesthetics.

54
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:40,980
 So this is a look at the modified ridge lap.

55
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:46,580
 You can see instead of wrapping around the buckle and lingual portion of the ridge, it's

56
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:54,340
 only sort of half way in contact with the buckle part of the ridge and the lingual part

57
00:04:54,340 --> 00:05:01,060
 is kind of more like the conical design where it peels off and isn't firmly attached and

58
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:03,620
 right up against the soft tissue.

59
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:10,780
 So this enables you to have some cleanseability, not as much as the conical or hygienic designs

60
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:13,500
 because there's a little bit more contact there.

61
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:15,900
 But you're talking about an anterior tooth.

62
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:20,420
 So aesthetics is certainly a consideration.

63
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:26,900
 So you don't obviously want to have a big gaping hole here and even just a little point

64
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:30,060
 contact isn't going to look very natural.

65
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:37,020
 So having this profile contact the soft tissue, you can actually make it appear as if it's

66
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:42,660
 coming out of the gingiva and make it a pretty aesthetic look.

67
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:48,780
 But even better for aesthetics and the most superior aesthetic design has to be the ovate

68
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:50,100
 pontic.

69
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:56,260
 This is used really only for anterior teeth and it provides superior aesthetics because

70
00:05:56,260 --> 00:06:03,580
 you actually embed the pontic into a little divot in the soft tissue and the ridge that

71
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:04,740
 you make.

72
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:08,980
 So this one actually requires a minor surgery.

73
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:15,340
 So you actually make this space so that the pontic can go into that little divot and it

74
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:23,420
 actually appears and it's coming out of the soft tissue just as a natural tooth would be

75
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:25,540
 emerging from the gingiva.

76
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:31,140
 We call that the emergence profile when a tooth is coming out of the gingiva in a certain

77
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:34,020
 way to make it appear very natural.

78
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:39,460
 So this one has the best aesthetics for that reason but it requires surgery and the proper

79
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:47,020
 dimensions of a dental ridge in order to make that phenomenon look and appear natural and

80
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:48,260
 aesthetic.

81
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:54,060
 Alright, so those are the five pontic designs I wanted to talk to you about.

82
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:58,940
 But we also have the certain connector designs that I want to mention.

83
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:04,540
 The connector is connecting the retainer to the pontic.

84
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:07,180
 So for connector designs we don't have five to choose from.

85
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:13,700
 We only have really two main categories, those being rigid and non-rigid.

86
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:19,180
 So the rigid connector is either cast in one piece or soldered together.

87
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:26,140
 So if we look at this image here, these connectors we can think of as being rigid.

88
00:07:26,140 --> 00:07:31,940
 You can't break them apart, they're stuck together, either cast all in one piece of

89
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:39,580
 metal or these have been soldered together so you can't remove them from each other.

90
00:07:39,580 --> 00:07:46,980
 The non-rigid connector design is shown right here in this image where you can actually

91
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:52,260
 put them together like puzzle pieces and take them apart.

92
00:07:52,260 --> 00:08:00,980
 So this male female kind of design is indicated when it's impossible to obtain a common path

93
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:05,580
 of insertion between the abutments.

94
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:11,580
 So we see a pretty good example of this in this image because the path of insertion is

95
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:15,180
 along the long axis of the prep.

96
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:18,580
 So that would be about the path of insertion for this one.

97
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:25,060
 Because this tooth is tipped and let's just say you can see how the prep has been made

98
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:31,780
 along the long axis of this tooth, the path of insertion for that crown would be at about

99
00:08:31,780 --> 00:08:33,380
 that angle.

100
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:39,820
 So now we have a discrepancy between this very up and down seating and this sort of oblique

101
00:08:39,820 --> 00:08:41,780
 path of insertion.

102
00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:52,940
 So if we had this all as one big piece with 1, 2, 3 rigid connectors, probably we might

103
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:59,980
 not even be able to seat that bridge because of the discrepancy between the path of insertions.

104
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:07,380
 So if this is an issue then we pull out the non-rigid connector design in order to first

105
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:15,540
 we can seat this one and then since this male connector can slide into this female connector

106
00:09:15,540 --> 00:09:21,300
 in a very up and down path of insertion then it's in line with this path of insertion and

107
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:28,060
 we can fit the bridge in two steps, putting this crown in first and then followed by this

108
00:09:28,060 --> 00:09:31,740
 bridge component second.

109
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:39,500
 So that's the use of having a rigid and non-rigid connector design and connectors for porcelain

110
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:47,660
 fuse to metal bridges as a sort of guideline should have a minimum of about 3mm of height.

111
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:53,140
 This is so that you have enough strength of the connector so that it doesn't fracture.

112
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:58,740
 Of course if you could afford to have more than that, that's great because increasing

113
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:05,260
 the connector height would increase the strength of the connector due to cervical occlusal

114
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:08,460
 reinforcement.

115
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:14,660
 And by cervical occlusal reinforcement I just mean the vertical dimension from cervical

116
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:20,460
 to occlusal, the thicker that is, the less likely it is to fracture.

117
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:25,540
 Alright thanks so much for watching everyone, I really appreciate all the support, please

118
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:30,900
 stay tuned for the next video in our fixed prosthodontic series, thanks so much for watching

119
00:10:30,900 --> 00:10:32,060
 and I'll see you all next time.

